<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-10-30T15:08:33Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Bayern Munich's Kenya friendly highlights Africa's growing pull for global sport</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d9a0cfbaf3bf60572b1cd8c17871e8b7d7496a872cc8b16e56331bb7aae603c1</id><updated>2026-10-30T15:08:33Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T13:19:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/bayern-munich-s-kenya-friendly-highlights-africa-s-growing-pull-for-global-sport/a-79796753--c6f4b50cf1c0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/bayern-munich-s-kenya-friendly-highlights-africa-s-growing-pull-for-global-sport/a-79796753?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Bayern Munich are to open Nairobi's new Raila Odinga International Stadium against Ajax as Kenya prepares for AFCON 2027. The match highlights Africa's growing sports ambitions — and questions over cost and legacy.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Human trafficking victims stranded in Guinea-Bissau</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b40c3cff8aebde971c3e21571596b51e98f331c71c563e73c5d2458fb0992ae8</id><updated>2026-10-30T15:07:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:20:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/human-trafficking-victims-stranded-in-guinea-bissau/a-79785558--af9688fcc3e2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/human-trafficking-victims-stranded-in-guinea-bissau/a-79785558?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Dozens of West Africans lured to Guinea-Bissau with promises of jobs and migration to Europe say they were exploited by a suspected trafficking network. Now many cannot afford to return home.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>One year on, families seek justice after election violence</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2bea710a1f953722b357ece6bde9399a10f5879df2430f888e1fa59e309f8913</id><updated>2026-10-30T15:07:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-28T16:30:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/one-year-on-families-seek-justice-after-election-violence/a-79779105--19ee0afa750b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/one-year-on-families-seek-justice-after-election-violence/a-79779105?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">One year after Tanzania's deadly post-election violence, families are still seeking answers. A government inquiry recorded 518 deaths. DW research has documented apparent use of excessive force by security forces.</summary><category term="World" /></entry></feed>
